chat-nojev
==========

This repository is a derivative work. It takes the Jev 聊天助手 (Jev Chat
Assistant) conversation copilot and replaces its separate judgment model
(TypeSafe Jev) with a single call to an ordinary LLM: the same model that
drafts the candidate replies also answers the judgment questions, picks the
best candidate and scores the others. The question set, the option keys and
their descriptions, and the 0-9 tension criteria are carried over unchanged
from upstream; only the producer of those values differs. Because one model now
both writes and rates the candidates, the `confidence` and `probabilities`
values it returns are the model's own self-report and are not calibrated the
way the upstream judgment model's were.

Two things beyond the producer differ. The three choice questions are answered
with a short phrase in the language of the conversation instead of one of the
fixed English keys, because a general model does not need a closed label set
the way a classifier does; the keys and descriptions still frame the questions,
but `app/overlay.py`'s table translating those keys into fixed Chinese display
text (`_CHOICES`) has been removed and the panel shows the model's own wording.
And the settings page has one model section instead of two, the judgment
provider having no counterpart here.

Everything else in `windows/` is the upstream Windows application.

`android/` is the same change applied to the Android original: one call to an
ordinary LLM replaces the separate judgment model there too. The judgment
result stays the typed `Analysis` / `Choice` / `Score` object the overlay reads
field by field, the question set is unchanged, the overlay's three tables
translating the classifier's English keys into Chinese are removed for the same
reason `_CHOICES` was, and the two parallel analysis calls become one. The
upstream `apk/` binary was not copied. See `android/docs/EQUIVALENCE.md`.

`macos/` is the same change applied to the macOS sister project, which is not a
port of either of the others and asks a different question set: two questions
in Chinese, an intent over eight labels and a risk over ten levels. It had two
judgment models — a local decoder (`Mapika/decider-2b`) and the cloud TypeSafe
Jev — and a third call that ranked the candidates; all three are now the same
call that writes the replies, one per tone, run concurrently as upstream runs
them. The verdict the panel reads keeps its keys and its values. **The local
judge is deleted**, and with it the ability to keep that judgment entirely on
the machine, with no key and no network call leaving it — generation always
needed a remote model, so this never made the app work offline, but it did
keep the judgment private. That is a loss of function, not only of a model,
and it is recorded at the top of `macos/src/questions.py` along with what it
would take to restore. The intent
is answered as a short phrase in the language of the conversation instead of
one of the eight fixed labels, so the panel shows the model's own wording; the
labels and their descriptions still frame the question. The `actions` row, a
static table keyed by that label upstream (`ACTION_MAP`, with a comment saying
no generation is involved), is written by the model in the same call, and the
upstream table is carried over verbatim as the example the prompt shows it. The
validation the upstream judge client performed on its way out — the label
lookup, the numeric coercions and the substitutions for a missing value — is
not reproduced in the adapter; the panel guards each value where it renders it
instead. The settings page has two model sections instead of three, and
`torch`, `transformers`, `huggingface-hub` and `laya` are no longer
dependencies. See `macos/docs/EQUIVALENCE.md`.

